# ============================================================
# calendar.txt   -   one event per line: YYYY-MM-DD  description  [@time]
#
# Tags (:birthday: :health: :car: :family: :subscription: :bill: :trial: ...)
# are OPTIONAL; add one when an entry is worth flagging or filtering later.
# 
# Space-separated, one token each, NOT chained (:a:b:), as in LIFE.TXT:
#                       2026-10-06  Sam birthday :birthday: :family:
#
# Tag style: calendar.txt and LIFE.TXT both use colon-delimited :tag: tokens,
# space-separated and NOT chained - one syntax to remember, and one grep
# works across both files.
#
# In calendar.txt the @ sign is reserved for time (see below), so it means 
# one thing only. (Decided when @time was added: @ was about to do two 
# jobs - tag and time - so tags moved to :tag: rather
# than keep a different style per file. 
#
# Chaining stays off, as in LIFE.TXT.
#
# Time: an optional @time token puts same-day events in time order in agenda.
# Forms: @10am  @1pm-3pm  @11-11:15am  @13:30  (24h when no am/pm).
# Only the start time orders; untimed entries list first. 
# A time always starts with a # digit, and @ is used for nothing else.
# Position on the line does not matter:
#                       2026-10-06  Dentist @9:30am-10:30am :health:
#
# View:  agenda     (today highlighted + upcoming; see: agenda --help)
# Edit:  vi <path>/calendar.txt   - type a dated line anywhere, save.
#
# Layout: entries are kept interleaved in DATE order, so reading the raw file
# (cat calendar.txt) shows your timeline - what is coming up, next by next.
#
# agenda sorts by date, then @time, at read time regardless, so order is for
# YOUR reading. (The vi re-sort below orders a day's entries by text, not
# time - agenda still shows them in time order.)
#
#   Re-sort after edits:  in vi, put the cursor on the first dated line and run
#                         :.,$!sort      (sorts that line to end; leaves this
#                         header alone; leading YYYY-MM-DD sorts as plain text)
#   Grouped view by type: grep :birthday: <path>/calendar.txt
#   Annual birthdays:     set the full date to the next occurrence; bump the
#                         year in place as each passes, then re-sort as above.
#
# NOTE: This is a template. 
# The example dates below are illustrative - replace them with your own.
# (Being in the past, they will not show as upcoming in agenda until you edit them.)
# 
#
# NOTE: Location is adjustable. agenda reads it from $LIFE_DIR (default ~/life,
# so ~/life/calendar.txt). 
# Set LIFE_DIR to point elsewhere, e.g.
# /mnt/usbflashdrive/life/calendar.txt on a machine using a USB drive.
# ============================================================

2026-06-26  :subscription:  ExampleWeekly - weekly (re-seed as it passes)
2026-06-30  :trial:  Example Service trial ends 2026-07-01 - cancel or keep?
2026-07-02  :health:  Dr. - annual checkup @9:30am
2026-07-03  :subscription:  ExampleWeekly - weekly
2026-07-12  :subscription:  Netflix - monthly. Still want it?
2026-07-12  :subscription:  TSN - monthly. Still want it?
2026-07-15  :bill:  Mortgage payment due (15th monthly)
2026-09-05  :birthday: :family:  John Vega (b. 1980)
2027-03-12  :birthday: :family:  Jane Doe (b. 1973)
